{
  "name": "regex-case-pairs-jdk17-backreferences",
  "description": "Dart's RegExp folds a letter under `i` by its own Unicode 16 case classes. Platforms with an older Unicode lack some of them: Linux Swift 5.10's ICU and JDK 17 lack the Unicode 14 pairs (the Glagolitic caudate chrivi U+2C2F/U+2C5F, the Latin old Polish o U+A7C0/U+A7C1, closed insular g U+A7D0/U+A7D1, middle Scots s U+A7D6/U+A7D7 and sigmoid s U+A7D8/U+A7D9, and the Vithkuqi a \ud801\udd70 U+10570/\ud801\udd97 U+10597), and Linux Swift 5.10 and 6.2 and JDK 17 and 21 lack Garay (the a \ud803\udd50 U+10D50/\ud803\udd70 U+10D70, Unicode 16); the SDKs fold these by Dart's classes there too. A literal letter matches its other case with `gi` and `giu`, either way round: U+2C2F with `gi` on U+2C5F, U+2C5F with `giu` on U+2C2F, U+A7C1 with `gi` on U+A7C0, U+A7D0 with `giu` on U+A7D1 and U+A7D9 with `gi` on U+A7D8 each give \"#\". Above U+FFFF a letter folds only with `u`: \ud803\udd50 with `giu`, and `(?i:\ud803\udd50)` with `gu`, on \ud803\udd70 give \"#\", as does \ud801\udd97 with `giu` on \ud801\udd70, while \ud803\udd50 with `gi` leaves \ud803\udd70 unchanged. A class folds the same way: `[\ua7d6]` with `gi` on U+A7D7, `[\ud803\udd70]` with `giu` on \ud803\udd50 and the range `[\ud803\udd50-\ud803\udd51]` with `giu` on \ud803\udd70 give \"#\", and the negated class `[^\ud803\udd50]` with `giu` leaves \ud803\udd70 unchanged. So does a backreference: `(\u2c2f)\\1` with `gi` on U+2C2F U+2C5F, `(.)\\1` with `giu` on U+A7C0 U+A7C1 and with `gi` on U+A7D9 U+A7D8, `(.)\\1` with `giu` on \ud803\udd70\ud803\udd50, `(?i:(\ud803\udd50)\\1)` with `gu` on \ud803\udd50\ud803\udd70 and `(\ud801\udd70)\\1` with `giu` on \ud801\udd70\ud801\udd97 give \"#\", and `(\ud803\udd50)\\1` with `giu` on \ud803\udd50\ud803\udd70z gives \"#z\". With the replacement \"<$1>\" the group reads the input's own letter back: `(\u2c5f)\\1` with `giu` on U+2C2F U+2C5F gives \"<\u2c2f>\" and `(.)\\1` with `giu` on \ud803\udd70\ud803\udd50 gives \"<\ud803\udd70>\". JDK 17's case-insensitive backreference never repeats a capture above U+FFFF, so on JDK 17 the SDK writes such a backreference exact, after giving the letters of each of Dart's case classes in the input one shared stand-in. With `giu`, `(.)\\1` on \ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00 gives \"#\", on \ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00x \"#x\" and with \"<$1>\" \"<\ud83d\ude00>\"; `(\ud83d\ude00)\\1` on x\ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00 gives \"x#\"; `(?<n>.)\\k<n>` on \ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00 and `\\w(.)\\1` on a\ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00 give \"#\"; `(.)\\1` on the Deseret long I \ud801\udc00\ud801\udc28 (U+10400 U+10428), capital then small, still gives \"#\"; and `(?i:(.)\\1)` with `gu` on x\ud83d\ude00\ud83d\ude00 gives \"x#\". Every replace-mode regex puts \"#\" for each match unless its replacement is named above. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
